-- | Week 3 — investor onboarding and credential-gating, reworked per
-- Alex's direction after reviewing the first draft of this module: base
-- the flow on DA Registry's own credential-gating pattern rather than
-- inventing a different shape.
--
-- Two things changed from the first draft:
--
-- 1. Self-onboarding, propose-accept -- the same shape DA Registry
--    itself uses for a Provider/Registrar onboarding (the party being
--    onboarded proposes, the authority accepts), rather than admin
--    unilaterally creating a Pending record on the investor's behalf.
--    That also means there's no separate Pending status to track
--    anymore -- an InvestorOnboardingRequest's mere existence on the
--    ledger *is* "pending review," so InvestorCredential only ever gets
--    created already-decided.
--
-- 2. Renamed InvestorRegistration -> InvestorCredential, to actually
--    use DA Registry's own vocabulary. The point isn't cosmetic: an
--    Approved InvestorCredential is meant to be the same *kind* of
--    on-ledger fact a DA Registry Instrument Configuration's credential
--    gate checks (see Ozmium.Subscription) -- so when real DA Registry
--    integration happens (Week 4+), this doesn't need translating, just
--    pointing at.
--
-- Manual approval is still exactly what it was -- admin decides by
-- hand, no live KYC provider wired in (none is locked in yet). The
-- clean seam for one, per Alex: `investor : Party` is a real Canton
-- PartyID with no indirection through an internal id, so a future
-- AddressBook Identity Mapper (Canton PartyID -> DFNS WalletID) can
-- read it straight off any Approved InvestorCredential. And
-- InvestorCredential_Reinstate below (paired with _Suspend, same shape
-- as AttestorRegistry_Revoke/_Renew) is the seam for automation to flip
-- someone back to Approved later without a whole new onboarding cycle.
module Ozmium.Investor where

import Ozmium.AddressBook

-- | Prefixed for the same reason as everywhere else in this codebase --
-- unqualified imports everywhere means Approved/Rejected would collide
-- with SubscriptionStatus's constructors the moment a file needs both
-- in scope (Checkpoint7 does).
data InvestorCredentialStatus
  = InvestorApproved
  | InvestorRejected
  | InvestorSuspended
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The investor's own proposal to onboard with `fund` -- signed by the
-- investor, same as a DA Registry Provider/Registrar self-onboards by
-- proposing themselves rather than being unilaterally registered by the
-- authority above them. No credential exists yet at this point.
--
-- `admin` has to be a field here (and an observer), not just a loose
-- choice argument on Approve/Reject below -- Daml's privacy model means
-- a party has to actually be a stakeholder to fetch or exercise a
-- choice on a contract at all. The first draft passed `admin` in as a
-- choice argument without making them an observer, which compiled fine
-- but failed at runtime with "contract not visible to the reading
-- parties" the moment admin tried to act on it -- caught by the real
-- `dpm test` run, not by the type checker.
template InvestorOnboardingRequest
  with
    investor         : Party
    fund             : Party
    admin            : Party
    submittedOn      : Date
    applicationNotes : Text  -- free text, whatever the investor wants reviewers to see; "" is fine
  where
    signatory investor
    observer fund, admin

    choice InvestorOnboardingRequest_Approve : ContractId InvestorCredential
      with
        effectiveOn : Date
        notes       : Text
      controller admin
      do
        create InvestorCredential with
          admin
          fund
          investor
          status = InvestorApproved
          reviewNotes = notes
          lastUpdatedOn = effectiveOn

    choice InvestorOnboardingRequest_Reject : ContractId InvestorCredential
      with
        effectiveOn : Date
        notes       : Text
      controller admin
      do
        create InvestorCredential with
          admin
          fund
          investor
          status = InvestorRejected
          reviewNotes = notes
          lastUpdatedOn = effectiveOn

-- | The credential itself -- the on-ledger fact Ozmium.Subscription's
-- Accept choice checks today, and the shape a real DA Registry
-- credential gate would check once that integration exists. Scoped per
-- fund rather than per-asset, same reasoning as the first draft: an
-- investor's compliance standing is about their relationship with the
-- issuer, not with any one token, so one credential covers every asset
-- that fund offers.
template InvestorCredential
  with
    admin         : Party
    fund          : Party
    investor      : Party
    status        : InvestorCredentialStatus
    reviewNotes   : Text
    lastUpdatedOn : Date
  where
    signatory admin
    observer fund, investor

    -- Pulls a previously-Approved investor's access immediately,
    -- independent of anything else about them -- same idea as
    -- AttestorRegistry_Revoke pulling an attestor's license immediately
    -- rather than waiting for a natural lapse.
    choice InvestorCredential_Suspend : ContractId InvestorCredential
      with
        effectiveOn : Date
        notes       : Text
      controller admin
      do
        create this with status = InvestorSuspended, reviewNotes = notes, lastUpdatedOn = effectiveOn

    -- Lifts a Suspended credential back to Approved -- the issue that
    -- caused the suspension got resolved, or (once a live KYC provider
    -- is wired in) their automation re-clears the investor. Same
    -- "doesn't need a whole new proposal, just a re-decision" shape as
    -- AttestorRegistry_Renew.
    choice InvestorCredential_Reinstate : ContractId InvestorCredential
      with
        effectiveOn : Date
        notes       : Text
      controller admin
      do
        create this with status = InvestorApproved, reviewNotes = notes, lastUpdatedOn = effectiveOn

    -- Links this Approved investor's Canton PartyID to a DFNS wallet --
    -- see Ozmium.AddressBook for why the resulting record lives in its
    -- own module rather than here. Checking status == InvestorApproved
    -- explicitly (not just "this contract exists") is what actually
    -- keeps a Suspended or Rejected investor from ever getting linked --
    -- nothing about this choice's controller/signatory shape alone would
    -- stop that otherwise.
    choice InvestorCredential_LinkDfnsWallet : ContractId AddressBookEntry
      with
        effectiveOn  : Date
        dfnsWalletId : Text
        linkNotes    : Text
      controller admin
      do
        assertMsg ("Investor's credential is not Approved (status: " <> show status <> ")") (status == InvestorApproved)
        create AddressBookEntry with
          admin
          fund
          investor
          dfnsWalletId
          linkedOn = effectiveOn
          status = AddressBookLinked
          notes = linkNotes
